##### MF2: RESONANCE PARAMETERS                                   8834 1451  233
                                                                  8834 1451  234
- MT151    : Resonance parameters                                 8834 1451  235
                                                                  8834 1451  236
Details about the origin of the resonance parameters for this     8834 1451  237
nuclide are given above in this description. In general, both a   8834 1451  238
Resolved and an Unresolved Resonance Range are given.             8834 1451  239
                                                                  8834 1451  240
##### MF3: REACTION CROSS SECTIONS                                8834 1451  241
                                                                  8834 1451  242
All the data present in the following MT-sections have been       8834 1451  243
calculated with TALYS, possibly normalized to experimental data.  8834 1451  244
If the maximal cross section in an excitation function over the   8834 1451  245
whole energy range does not exceed 1.e-9 b, the MT-number is not  8834 1451  246
included. Cross sections lower than 1.e-20 b are assumed to have  8834 1451  247
no physical meaning and are set to zero. All MT numbers extend up 8834 1451  248
to the highest incident energy.                                   8834 1451  249
The following reaction channels/MT numbers are generally included:8834 1451  250
                                                                  8834 1451  251
- MT1      : Total cross section                                  8834 1451  252
                                                                  8834 1451  253
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range,    8834 1451  254
or partly reproduced using experimental data.                     8834 1451  255
                                                                  8834 1451  256
- MT2      : Elastic scattering cross section                     8834 1451  257
                                                                  8834 1451  258
Obtained by subtracting the non-elastic cross section from the    8834 1451  259
total cross section.                                              8834 1451  260
                                                                  8834 1451  261
- MT3      : Non-elastic cross section                            8834 1451  262
                                                                  8834 1451  263
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range.    8834 1451  264
                                                                  8834 1451  265
- MT4      : Total inelastic cross section                        8834 1451  266
                                                                  8834 1451  267
Sum of MT=51-91.                                                  8834 1451  268
                                                                  8834 1451  269
- MT5      : (n,anything) cross section                           8834 1451  270
                                                                  8834 1451  271
MT5 contains the reactions which can not be stored in any other   8834 1451  272
MT-number. The information of MF3/MT5 can be combined with        8834 1451  273
MF6/MT5 to obtain residual production cross sections particle     8834 1451  274
production cross sections and (double-)differential cross         8834 1451  275
sections.                                                         8834 1451  276
                                                                  8834 1451  277
- MT16     : (n,2n) cross section                                 8834 1451  278
- MT17     : (n,3n) cross section                                 8834 1451  279
- MT22     : (n,na) cross section                                 8834 1451  280
- MT28     : (n,np) cross section                                 8834 1451  281
- MT51-90  : (n,n') cross section for 1st-40th excited state      8834 1451  282
- MT91     : (n,n') continuum cross section                       8834 1451  283
- MT102    : (n,gamma) cross section                              8834 1451  284
- MT103    : (n,p) cross section                                  8834 1451  285
- MT104    : (n,d) cross section                                  8834 1451  286
- MT105    : (n,t) cross section                                  8834 1451  287
- MT106    : (n,h) cross section                                  8834 1451  288
- MT107    : (n,a) cross section                                  8834 1451  289
- MT600-610: (n,p) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       8834 1451  290
- MT649    : (n,p) continuum cross section                        8834 1451  291
- MT650-655: (n,d) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        8834 1451  292
- MT699    : (n,d) continuum cross section                        8834 1451  293
- MT700-705: (n,t) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        8834 1451  294
- MT749    : (n,t) continuum cross section                        8834 1451  295
- MT750-755: (n,h) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        8834 1451  296
- MT799    : (n,h) continuum cross section                        8834 1451  297
- MT800-810: (n,a) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       8834 1451  298
- MT849    : (n,a) continuum cross section                        8834 1451  299
                                                                  8834 1451  300
##### MF4: ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS OF SECONDARY PARTICLES           8834 1451  301
                                                                  8834 1451  302
We use MF4 for the neutron scattering angular distribution for    8834 1451  303
discrete levels.                                                  8834 1451  304
                                                                  8834 1451  305
- MT2      : Elastic angular distribution                         8834 1451  306
                                                                  8834 1451  307
The flag LTT=3 is used to indicate a switch at some high energy,  8834 1451  308
generally 50 MeV, from a Legendre representation to a tabulated   8834 1451  309
representation. For incident energies below this energy, the      8834 1451  310
Legendre coefficients are given on a sufficiently precise energy  8834 1451  311
grid. They are a sum of calculated Legendre coefficients for      8834 1451  312
compound nucleus and shape-elastic scattering. For higher         8834 1451  313
incident energies, relative angular distributions are tabulated   8834 1451  314
on an angular grid.                                               8834 1451  315
                                                                  8834 1451  316
- MT51-70  : (n,n') angular distribution for 1st-20th state       8834 1451  317
- MT600-610: (n,p) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        8834 1451  318
- MT650-655: (n,d) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         8834 1451  319
- MT700-705: (n,t) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         8834 1451  320
- MT750-755: (n,h) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         8834 1451  321
- MT800-810: (n,a) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        8834 1451  322
                                                                  8834 1451  323
##### MF5: FISSION NEUTRON SPECTRUM                               8834 1451  324
                                                                  8834 1451  325
The data for the fission neutron spectrum are provided by the     8834 1451  326
TANES code[kon12]. Both prompt and delayed spectra are given.     8834 1451  327
                                                                  8834 1451  328
- MT18     : Prompt fission neutron spectrum                      8834 1451  329
                                                                  8834 1451  330
- MT455    : Delayed fission neutron spectrum                     8834 1451  331
                                                                  8834 1451  332
##### MF6: PRODUCT ENERGY-ANGLE DISTRIBUTIONS                     8834 1451  333
                                                                  8834 1451  334
In MF6 we store all secondary energy, angle, and energy-angle     8834 1451  335
distributions, as well as all residual and discrete + continuum   8834 1451  336
photon production cross sections for channels other than capture  8834 1451  337
(MT102) and reactions to discrete levels (MT51,etc.).             8834 1451  338
All data are generated with TALYS.                                8834 1451  339
                                                                  8834 1451  340
- MT5      : (n,anything) yields and energy-angle distributions   8834 1451  341
                                                                  8834 1451  342
MT5 contains, in this order,                                      8834 1451  343
For neutrons up to alpha particles and photons:                   8834 1451  344
- the production yields per particle for the whole energy range   8834 1451  345
- the secondary energy-angle distributions per particles which are8834 1451  346
  not represented by explicit MT numbers. On a secondary energy   8834 1451  347
  grid the relative emission spectra are given together with the  8834 1451  348
  parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular              8834 1451  349
  distributions. Inelastic scattering cross sections for discrete 8834 1451  350
  states have been broadened and added to the continuum spectra.  8834 1451  351
For each residual product:                                        8834 1451  352
- the production yields for the whole energy range.               8834 1451  353
- the recoil                                                      8834 1451  354
These distributions can be multiplied with MF3/MT5 to get         8834 1451  355
the production cross sections and (double-)differential cross     8834 1451  356
sections.                                                         8834 1451  357
                                                                  8834 1451  358
- MT16     : (n,2n) energy-angle distr. and photon production     8834 1451  359
                                                                  8834 1451  360
First, for each type of outgoing particle, the (trivial) integer  8834 1451  361
particle yields are given. Next, on a sufficiently dense incident 8834 1451  362
energy grid the secondary energy-angle distributions are          8834 1451  363
specified by means of the relative emission spectra and the       8834 1451  364
parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular distributions. 8834 1451  365
Next, the photon yield is tabulated as a function of incident     8834 1451  366
energy. For each incident energy, the photon production is given  8834 1451  367
for all discrete gamma lines present in the final nucleus. A      8834 1451  368
continuum photon distribution is added to this. We assume         8834 1451  369
isotropy for all produced gamma rays.                             8834 1451  370
                                                                  8834 1451  371
For the following MT-numbers, the same procedure as for MT16 is   8834 1451  372
followed:                                                         8834 1451  373
                                                                  8834 1451  374
- MT17 : (n,3n) energy-angle distr. and photon production         8834 1451  375
- MT22 : (n,na) energy-angle distr. and photon production         8834 1451  376
- MT28 : (n,np) energy-angle distr. and photon production         8834 1451  377
- MT91 : (n,n') continuum energy-angle distr. and phot. prod.     8834 1451  378
- MT649: (n,p) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     8834 1451  379
- MT699: (n,d) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     8834 1451  380
- MT749: (n,t) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     8834 1451  381
- MT809: (n,h) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     8834 1451  382
- MT849: (n,a) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     8834 1451  383
                                                                  8834 1451  384
##### MF8: RADIOACTIVE DECAY DATA                                 8834 1451  385
                                                                  8834 1451  386
For reactions to isomers, MF8 designates where the information    8834 1451  387
for isomeric versus ground state production, or residual          8834 1451  388
production can be found, i.e. for each MT number it points to     8834 1451  389
either MF6, MF9 or MF10.                                          8834 1451  390
                                                                  8834 1451  391
##### MF9: MULTIPLICITIES FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES  8834 1451  392
                                                                  8834 1451  393
MF9 is used for isomer production by non-threshold reactions.     8834 1451  394
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    8834 1451  395
as isomer.                                                        8834 1451  396
                                                                  8834 1451  397
##### MF10: CROSS SECTIONS FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES 8834 1451  398
                                                                  8834 1451  399
MF10 is used for isomer production by threshold reactions.        8834 1451  400
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    8834 1451  401
as isomer.                                                        8834 1451  402
                                                                  8834 1451  403
##### MF12: PHOTON PRODUCTION YIELD DATA                          8834 1451  404
                                                                  8834 1451  405
MF12 is used for the photon production for two types of reactions:8834 1451  406
- Reactions to discrete levels (MT51, etc.) For this transition   8834 1451  407
  probabilities are used (LO=2)                                   8834 1451  408
- Photons from neutron capture (MT102). Here multiplicities are   8834 1451  409
  given                                                           8834 1451  410
                                                                  8834 1451  411
##### MF14: PHOTON ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                          8834 1451  412
                                                                  8834 1451  413
All photon production is considered as isotropic                  8834 1451  414
                                                                  8834 1451  415
##### MF15: PHOTON ENERGY SPECTRA                                 8834 1451  416
                                                                  8834 1451  417
For capture (MT102), the photon energy spectrum is given.         8834 1451  418
                                                                  8834 1451  419
##### MF31: COVARIANCES OF AVERAGE FISSION NEUTRON DATA           8834 1451  420
                                                                  8834 1451  421
The covariance data for the number of fission neutrons are        8834 1451  422
provided by the TAFIS code[kon12]. On the basis of rather simple  8834 1451  423
assumptions a full covariance file for the total nubar is         8834 1451  424
obtained.                                                         8834 1451  425
                                                                  8834 1451  426
##### MF32: COVARIANCES OF RESONANCE PARAMETERS                   8834 1451  427
                                                                  8834 1451  428
In MF32, we store the covariances of resonance parameters,        8834 1451  429
and more details are given above in this description.             8834 1451  430
                                                                  8834 1451  431
##### MF33: COVARIANCES OF NEUTRON CROSS SECTIONS                 8834 1451  432
                                                                  8834 1451  433
In MF33, we store the covariances of most, but not all, reaction  8834 1451  434
channels that are given in MF3. All covariances are generated     8834 1451  435
with the TASMAN code [kon12], which generates TALYS runs using    8834 1451  436
a Monte Carlo parameter method.                                   8834 1451  437
For each MT-number, the information consists of:                  8834 1451  438
1. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Ea (variances)                8834 1451  439
2. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Eb (correlations for the      8834 1451  440
   same reaction channel between different energies)              8834 1451  441
3. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Ea (correlations between      8834 1451  442
   different reaction channels for the same energy)               8834 1451  443
4. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Eb (correlations between      8834 1451  444
   different reaction channels for different energies)            8834 1451  445
Classes 1 and 2 are covered by NI-type subsubsections with        8834 1451  446
flag LB=5, followed by a LB=8 section which contains              8834 1451  447
the cross section variances multiplied by 1.e-3. Next, classes 3  8834 1451  448
and 4 are covered by flag LB=6 to account for the different       8834 1451  449
energy grids that two correlating MT-sections may have. Since     8834 1451  450
the total correlation matrix is fully symmetric, we only need to  8834 1451  451
include the correlation elements between different MT-numbers     8834 1451  452
once, and we do that in the subsection with the lowest of the     8834 1451  453
two MT-numbers. We include covariance information for all the     8834 1451  454
total cross sections, the lumped partial cross sections and the   8834 1451  455
individual inelastic cross sections per level (MT51,...). We do   8834 1451  456
not include covariances for MT600 and higher MT-numbers. For the  8834 1451  457
most important channels, we include cross-channel correlations.   8834 1451  458
                                                                  8834 1451  459
##### MF34: COVARIANCES OF ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                  8834 1451  460
                                                                  8834 1451  461
In MF34, we store the covariances of the elastic scattering       8834 1451  462
angular distributions that are given in MF4/MT2. All covariances  8834 1451  463
are generated with the TASMAN code [kon12], which generates       8834 1451  464
TALYS runs using a Monte Carlo parameter method. The tables are   8834 1451  465
restricted to MT2 and contain covariances for the first 6         8834 1451  466
Legendre coefficients. Both intra- and inter-correlations of the  8834 1451  467
various Legendre coefficients are given. As in MF33, the LB5      8834 1451  468
representation is used.                                           8834 1451  469
                                                                  8834 1451  470
##### MF35: COVARIANCES OF FISSION NEUTRON SPECTRUM               8834 1451  471
                                                                  8834 1451  472
The covariance data for fission neutron spectra are provided by   8834 1451  473
the TANES code[kon12]. The contents of MF35 are covariance data   8834 1451  474
between different outgoing energies of the neutron spectra.       8834 1451  475
                                                                  8834 1451  476
##### MF40: COVARIANCES OF ISOMER PRODUCTION                      8834 1451  477
                                                                  8834 1451  478
For covariances of ground state and isomeric cross sections, the  8834 1451  479
same procedures as for MF33 are used.                             8834 1451  480
                                                                  8834 1451  481
***** F I L E   C H E C K I N G   A N D   P R O C E S S I N G ****8834 1451  482
